1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Do hospital queues cost your job?

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Hello everyone and welcome to this new insight.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Today we will shed light on the real costs, the hidden ones of medical waiting lists.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
We will dive headlong into the data of a research that is fascinating to say the least,

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
published in the America Economic Journal.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
The objective is to analyze the study's original data and graphs to understand, in a nutshell,

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
what really happens while people wait their turn in the hospital.

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Let's start with part one, the waiting list conundrum.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Let's face it, the frustrating experience of having to wait months for orthopedic surgery

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
not urgent, such as perhaps hip or knee protection,

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
it's a story we know all too well.

12
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,000
But this brings us straight to a question that completely overturns our usual perspective.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
The real damage of these endless queues is only physical,

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
or it also devastatingly affects the ability to produce income

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
and to maintain a carguera, in short the damage is to the body or to the wallet.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Instinct and common sense suggest an answer that seems obvious.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
It's natural to think about making a patient wait a long time

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
it means condemning him to an inexorable physical decline.

19
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Joints that get worse, pain that increases,

20
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
perhaps the need to rush to the emergency room.

21
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Ah, perfect sense, right?

22
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
But the real question is, does the data really confirm this extinct of ours.

23
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
And here we enter the second part, where we discover the economists' trick.

24
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
In medicine, proving cause and effect is a real puzzle

25
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
because, because healthcare systems rightly tend to operate on those who are worse off first,

26
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
however, this creates a distortion in the data, because those who wait a short time

27
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
it is often those whose health is already precarious to begin with.

28
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
As it turns out, the authors of the study had a brilliant intuition

29
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
using a concept called con management or hospitalier.

30
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Instead of looking at how long the individual is waiting,

31
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
they measured the average waiting time of other patients,

32
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
in that same hospital at the exact same time.

33
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
This is pure chance and allows us to isolate the effect of waiting in a surgical way.

34
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Let's look at this line going up.

35
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
It clearly shows us a linear relationship.

36
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
If you arrive at the hospital at a time when the system is congested,

37
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
the individual wait will inevitably be longer.

38
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Using this congestion, as a statistical tool,

39
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
the researchers finally obtained clean, unassailable data

40
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
and above all completely untied

41
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
from any other clinical variables of the patient.

42
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Let's move on to the third part, with a title that says it all.

43
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Health in tact, job lost.

44
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Brace yourselves because the data here debunks the main false myth

45
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
with a crazy twist.

46
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Incredibly, longer waits do not lead to a boom in visits at all

47
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
by the GP, nor do they increase the probability

48
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
of emergency hospitalizations in the following five years.

49
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Overall baseline health holds up.

50
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
The real disaster occurs on a completely different battlefield.

51
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
The job offer takes a beating and the days of absence

52
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
they literally skyrocket.

53
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Analyzing the study's graphs, the contrast is astounding.

54
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
In the upper panels indicating the use of hospital facilities

55
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
we see practically flat lines, no significant increase.

56
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
But let's look down at the lower panels,

57
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
those who track sickness absences

58
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
and new entries on permanent disability.

59
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
There the lines surged dramatically in the years after the request for intervention.

60
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The wait has in fact not worn out the body,

61
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
but it has literally fractured the link with the world of work.

62
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
And if we want to translate these graphs into precise numbers,

63
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
the figures speak very clearly,

64
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
the study calculated that 10 extra days of waiting is enough

65
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
to cause 8.7 more days of absence from work,

66
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
spread over 5 years.

67
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
And it doesn't end there, there is a measurable increase

68
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
the risk of ending up with permanent disability.

69
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
All of this, I repeat because it is fundamental,

70
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
occurs without any significant increase in medical visits

71
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
or in the use of health services.

72
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Thus we enter the fourth part,

73
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
the trap of prolonged without.

74
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Now the spontaneous question arises,

75
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
but how is it possible that this effect affects your career?

76
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
The keystone is a concept that economists

77
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
they call State Dependence,

78
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
that is, the dependence on the current state.

79
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Simply put, the more time you spend

80
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
far from the office or factory,

81
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
the more the threads that bind us to the job market break.

82
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Skills become rusty, contacts are lost

83
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
and this distance creates a real barrier

84
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
both psychological and logistical.

85
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Getting back to normal becomes a mountain

86
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
increasingly steep to climb.

87
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
And we come to the discovery

88
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
which is perhaps the heart of the whole analysis.

89
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
This dizzying collapse of work

90
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
it doesn't affect everyone the same way.

91
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
In fact, the damage is almost relentless

92
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
exclusively on a super specific group.

93
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Let's talk about the patients who were already at home

94
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
leave due to illness.

95
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
At the exact moment when the doctor

96
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
he prescribed surgery for them.

97
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Look at this number 27.

98
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
It is a number that encompasses all gravity

99
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
of the situation for this group of people.

100
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
For those who are already at home sick

101
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
having to wait just 10 more days for the operation

102
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
generates 27 days in the long run

103
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
of additional work leave.

104
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Plus, it dramatically speeds up the transition

105
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
towards permanent disability.

106
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
It's a real chain reaction, unstoppable.

107
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
To counteract all this,

108
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
the data tells us that the patients who succeeded

109
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
still going to work, at the time of the visit,

110
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
despite having an orthopedic problem,

111
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
they do not suffer this long-term career damage.

112
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
For them, waiting a little longer means

113
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
just move the date of the operation,

114
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Don't make them jump out of the job market.

115
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
It is clear proof that the trap is sprung

116
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,000
by a fatal combination,

117
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
be already absent and suffer further waiting

118
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
placed by the system.

119
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
And we come to our fifth and final part.

120
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Who pays the final bill?

121
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Once you understand the dynamics on individuals,

122
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
It doesn't take much to understand that, on a large scale,

123
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
this financial avalanche overwhelms public coffers.

124
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
The real count of waiting lists

125
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
it's not just the expense of running the hospital.

126
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Maybe it was just that.

127
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
It is the extended payment of sickness benefits.

128
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
It is the opening of new, very heavy,

129
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
disability checks for people who otherwise

130
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
they would go back to work.

131
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
And she is, obviously, Maria di Tasse

132
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
that the state no longer collects

133
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
because these people lost their jobs.

134
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,000
A ruthless domino effect for public finances.

135
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
The calculations that the authors of the study put on the table

136
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
they are truly enlightening.

137
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
They show us black and white

138
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:27,000
and the resulting tax savings

139
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
from paying fewer benefits,

140
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
if only people were operated on in time,

141
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
surpass and by far,

142
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
the immediate costs to finance

143
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
those additional medical operations.

144
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Therefore, from a purely economic point of view,

145
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
cutting waiting lists is not a cost.

146
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
It is an investment that generates

147
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
a colossal net saving.

148
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
And we close this in-depth analysis

149
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
with a thought-provoking provocation.

150
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
In light of these data

151
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
we have to seriously question ourselves

152
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
on how efficient our health policies are.

153
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Tolerate and endless queues in hospitals

154
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
to try to save on today's budgets.

155
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
When then the direct consequence

156
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
is paying years of imballity benefits

157
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
and burn manpower tomorrow.

158
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Makes really logical sense.

159
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
I leave you with this question.

160
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
Thank you for following this analysis.

161
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Hopefully, we have offered a new perspective

162
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
about such a real and expensive problem.

163
